        "type": "tekst",
        "titel": "1.2 Individuality in social context: the strange case of suicide",
        "toetsstof": true,
        "tekst": "The perspective challenges common sense by showing that behaviour is not as individual as it feels. Daily life carries a heavy load of personal responsibility: we congratulate ourselves when things go well and kick ourselves when they do not.\n\n**Why suicide?** Because nothing seems more personal than the decision to take your own life. **Emile Durkheim (1858-1917)** chose it as a research topic precisely for that reason: if he could show that such an act is socially shaped, he would have made a strong case for sociology. And he did.\n\nDurkheim examined suicide records in and around France. Some categories of people were more likely than others to take their own lives: **men, Protestants, wealthy people and the unmarried** had higher rates than women, Roman Catholics and Jews, the poor, and married people. He deduced that the difference corresponded to the degree of **social integration**. **Low** suicide rates went with **strong social ties**; **high** rates with people who were **more socially isolated and individualistic**.\n\nThe logic in each case: men in male-dominated societies had more autonomy, and autonomy means lower integration; individualistic Protestants had fewer binding rituals than Catholics and Jews; the wealthy have more freedom of action than the poor; single people have weaker ties than married people.\n\n**Suicide in China.** A century later the analysis is still discussed, and the book shows where it does not fit. Table 1.1 gives rates per 100,000 by country and gender. In almost every country men are more likely to take their own lives, but **in China they are not**: women's suicides outnumber men's. China has 22 per cent of the world's people and about **40 per cent** of the world's suicides; it is the **fifth largest cause of death** there, with more than a quarter of a million deaths a year and two million attempts. In the West suicide is linked to **city** life; in China it is **three times higher in the countryside**, and it accounts for a third of all deaths among rural women.\n\n**Some figures from the book:** suicide rates rose by **60 per cent worldwide** in the half-century before the book was written; around **one million** people died by suicide in 2000, roughly one every 40 seconds; it is among the three leading causes of death for people aged 15 to 44; attempts are up to **20 times** more common than deaths.\n\nThe sociological point is not the numbers themselves. It is that **the most personal of acts shows stable social patterns**, and patterns call for a social explanation."
